From what you report, ending the relationship was not inherently cruel. If you concluded that marriage, a shared home, or raising a child together would fail, continuing because Colin had made sacrifices would have created larger costs later.

What the facts given do **not** establish:

- that ending it was the “harder” act; difficulty is not measurable from this account;
- that you were the only person facing reality;
- that you were a coward or a destroyer;
- whether your timing or manner caused avoidable harm.

The difficult point is sequence: Colin declined Chicago and agreed to a third round at your request, then you ended the relationship 3 weeks later. He may reasonably say he made consequential decisions while believing reconciliation remained possible. You can acknowledge that impact without claiming the breakup itself was wrong. Cruelty would depend more on whether you knowingly misled him, used the third round to postpone a decision already made, or ended matters with unnecessary contempt. You have not reported those facts.

A defensible statement for the meeting is:

> “I asked Colin to try again, and he made consequential choices in reliance on that request. Three weeks later, I concluded that continuing would be dishonest and ended the relationship. I accept responsibility for the impact and timing. I do not accept that remaining together out of guilt would have protected either of us—or a future child.”

My assessment: the evidence supports responsibility for a painful reversal, not the labels “coward” or “destroyer.” At the meeting, describe the sequence, acknowledge Colin’s concrete losses, and do not claim exclusive possession of the truth.
